+## Subtopics
+
+### [General NDC Ray Conversion](2429-general-ndc-ray-conversion.md)
+Handles arbitrary camera intrinsics and extrinsics by explicitly shifting ray origins to the near plane and projecting them based on the camera's focal length and image dimensions. It is primarily used in forward-facing scene reconstruction to handle large depth ranges efficiently.
+
+### [Blender NDC Convention](2430-blender-ndc-convention.md)
+Uses a fixed focal length and aspect ratio, assuming a specific NDC cube definition for synthetic Blender scenes. It does not handle arbitrary camera intrinsics as flexibly as the general method.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of rays. The transformation involves basic arithmetic operations per ray [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the transformed rays [G].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Allows rendering of scenes extending to infinity within a fixed bounding volume [S1].
-    *   Linear sampling in NDC space corresponds to linear sampling in disparity, which is often more natural for real-world scenes [S1].
-    *   Converts perspective rays to parallel rays in NDC space, simplifying some aspects of volume rendering or neural network input [S1].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Only suitable for forward-facing scenes [S1].
-    *   Requires careful handling of the near plane and ray shifting [S2, unofficial].
-    *   Not suitable for scenes with significant depth variation near the camera where disparity changes rapidly, potentially requiring dense sampling [G].
-*   **When to Use:** Ideal for forward-facing real-world scene reconstruction (e.g., LLFF, DeepVoxels) where the scene extends to infinity and a fixed bounding volume is desired [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for scenes with significant camera motion around the object (e.g., 360-degree rotation) or scenes with objects very close to the camera where disparity sampling becomes inefficient [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of rays. The transformation involves basic arithmetic operations per ray.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the transformed origins and directions.
+- **Performance:** The transformation is computationally inexpensive and is typically performed once during data loading or preprocessing [S4, S5, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Enables uniform sampling in disparity, which is critical for reconstructing real-world forward-facing scenes with large depth variations (e.g., LLFF dataset) [S1][S4, unofficial]. It maps the infinite depth range to a bounded NDC cube $[-1, 1]^3$ [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Only suitable for forward-facing scenes. Not appropriate for scenes with significant parallax or non-forward-facing camera movements [S1][S4, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** Use for real-world forward-facing datasets (e.g., LLFF) where depth extends to infinity and disparity sampling is desired [S1][S4, unofficial]. Do not use for synthetic Blender scenes or scenes with large camera rotations [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The NDC Ray Transformation was introduced in the context of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) for rendering real-world forward-facing scenes [S1]. The standard NeRF approach uses a synthetic scene bounded within a cube, but real-world captures can extend to infinity [S1]. The NDC space, commonly used in triangle rasterization pipelines, was adapted to map the depth range of real scenes into $[-1, 1]$ using disparity instead of metric depth [S1]. This allows the neural network to learn a bounded representation of the scene [S1]. The method was used in experiments with datasets like DeepVoxels and LLFF [S1].
+The NDC Ray Transformation was introduced in the NeRF paper (Mildenhall et al., 2020) to handle real-world forward-facing captures where the scene content exists between the closest point and infinity [S1]. It leverages the normalized device coordinate space commonly used in triangle rasterization pipelines, which preserves parallel lines and converts the z-axis to be linear in disparity [S1]. This allows the neural network to learn a bounded representation of the scene, simplifying the learning task for unbounded depth ranges [S1]. The method is widely adopted in subsequent NeRF variants for real-scene reconstruction, such as LLFF [S4, unofficial] and Instant-NGP [S3, S7, unofficial].
 
